characteristic-neutrino-mass-scale-08a21.interpretation:
A neutrino mass scale of $m_{
u} \sim 0.1 \, \mathrm{eV}/c^2$ is a crucial benchmark in particle physics and cosmology. In Type-I seesaw mechanisms, this small mass for the light, active neutrino ($m_{
u}$) is generated via its relationship with a very heavy, hypothetical right-handed neutrino of mass $M_R$. The relationship is approximately $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$, where $m_D$ is a Dirac mass term. If $m_D$ is assumed to be at the electroweak scale (e.g., related to the Higgs VEV, $v \approx 246$ GeV), then a light neutrino mass of $m_{
u} \approx 0.1$ eV implies a grand unification theory (GUT) scale for $M_R$, specifically $M_R \sim (246 \, 	ext{GeV})^2 / (0.1 \, 	ext{eV}) \approx 10^{14} - 10^{15}$ GeV. Cosmologically, the sum of all neutrino masses, $\sum m_{
u}$, influences the formation of large-scale structures in the universe by acting as hot dark matter. Current data from the cosmic microwave background (CMB) and galaxy surveys place an upper limit on $\sum m_{
u}$ (e.g., $\sum m_{
u} < 0.12$ eV from Planck+BAO), which is consistent with individual mass eigenstates being around or below the $0.1$ eV scale.
